507","idp_filedate":1795237643000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"A complex and large low pressure system includes three embedded low  pressure centers, one over the Gulf of America, another over  northern Florida, and a third a few hundred miles northeast of the  northwestern Bahamas. All of these systems are currently  non-tropical. However, the system over the western Atlantic still  has some potential to acquire subtropical or tropical  characteristics over the next day or two while it moves northward,  passing near the North Carolina and mid-Atlantic coasts on Sunday.  1. Although the chances of subtropical or tropical development continue  to decrease, this large weather system is still expected to produce  gusty winds, dangerous surf, rip currents, and coastal flooding  along portions of the southeast and mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into  early next week. In addition, heavy rainfall and the threat of flash  flooding will continue across portions of northeastern Florida  today, spreading northward over coastal portions of the Carolinas  and the mid-Atlantic through the weekend. Interests along the U.S.  East Coast should continue to monitor the progress of this system.  Additional information on this system, including Gale Warnings, can  be found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National Weather  Service, as well as products issued by your local National Weather  Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...20 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...low...20 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}